NAME
       chage - change or show a user's password ageing settings

SYNOPSIS
       chage [-m MIN] [-M MAX] [-W WARN] [-I INACTIVE] [-E DATE] [-d DATE] LOGIN
       chage -l LOGIN

DESCRIPTION
       chage sets the ageing fields of an account in /etc/shadow: how often
       the password must be changed, how long before that the user is warned,
       and when the whole account stops working. Only root may change them;
       a user may list their own with chage -l.

       The fields, all counted in days:

       last change
              The day the password was last changed. 0 forces a change at the
              next login.

       minimum (-m)
              Days that must pass before the user may change the password
              again (0 = any time).

       maximum (-M)
              Days a password stays valid; after that the user must choose a
              new one. 99999 (or -1) means never.

       warning (-W)
              Days before the expiry date that the user is warned at login.

       inactive (-I)
              Days after the password expired during which the user can
              still log in by changing it. After that the account is locked.

       account expiry (-E)
              The date the account stops working altogether, whatever the
              password.

       Dates are written YYYY-MM-DD (or a day number counted from
       1970-01-01). -1 clears a date or turns a limit off.

       New accounts take their defaults from PASS_MAX_DAYS, PASS_MIN_DAYS and
       PASS_WARN_AGE in /etc/login.defs. chage changes existing accounts
       only; edit login.defs for accounts created later.

OPTIONS
       -l, --list
              Show the settings in words.

       -m DAYS, --mindays DAYS
              Set the minimum number of days between changes.

       -M DAYS, --maxdays DAYS
              Set the maximum number of days a password is valid.

       -W DAYS, --warndays DAYS
              Set the warning period.

       -I DAYS, --inactive DAYS
              Set the inactive period after expiry. -1 turns it off.

       -E DATE, --expiredate DATE
              Set the account expiry date. -1 removes it.

       -d DATE, --lastday DATE
              Set the day of the last change. -d 0 makes the user choose a
              new password at the next login.

       -i, --iso8601
              With -l, print dates as YYYY-MM-DD.

EXAMPLES
       Make student change the password every 90 days, warned 7 days before:

           chage -M 90 -W 7 student

       Force a new password at the next login:

           chage -d 0 student

       Let the account of a contractor end on a date:

           chage -E 2026-12-31 contractor

       Check the result:

           chage -l student

FILES
       /etc/shadow
              Where the fields are stored (fields 3 to 8).

       /etc/login.defs
              Defaults for new accounts.

NOTES
       passwd -e LOGIN also expires a password at once, and usermod -e DATE
       sets the account expiry date.

SEE ALSO
       passwd(1), usermod(8), shadow(5), login.defs(5)
